### Introduction

`disks/html2.disk` isn't loaded by default -- it needs an explicit `@load`. Same spirit as `disks/css.disk` (`demos/css.disk`): markup is one plain struct (`Element`), and a single `Html_Formatter_Visitor` walks whatever tree you built and turns it into an HTML string. This coexists with `disks/html.disk` (`demos/html.disk`) -- a Div/P/H1/... per-tag Type with `html_*`/`css_*` declarations -- rather than replacing it; this one only builds a string, it doesn't hook into the server's live-render pipeline.
###
@load 'disks/html2.disk'

page := div([h1('Welcome to the beach'), p('Found supplies')])
@assert Html_Render.render(page) == '<div><h1>Welcome to the beach</h1><p>Found supplies</p></div>', 'Html_Render (compact) should render the tree as one unbroken string'


### Html_Format is the same visitor, pretty-printed

`Html_Render`/`Html_Format` are two shared instances of the same `Html_Formatter_Visitor` -- one compact (`minify := true`), one pretty (indented, one tag per line):
###
pretty := Html_Format.render(page)
@assert pretty == "<div>\n  <h1>Welcome to the beach</h1>\n  <p>Found supplies</p>\n</div>", 'Html_Format should indent one nested tag per line'


### Attributes are a real Array\Attribute, not a Dictionary

Every element constructor takes an `attributes` array of `Attribute(name, value)` structs as its second argument -- built the same way `Property` builds a CSS declaration, and in the same order they're given:
###
labeled := h1('Do not drink the water', [Attribute('id', 'warning'), Attribute('class', 'sign')])
@assert Html_Render.render(labeled) == '<h1 id="warning" class="sign">Do not drink the water</h1>', 'attributes should render in the order given'


### Void tags never get a closing tag

`br`, `img`, `input`, and friends never emit `</tag>`, in either mode -- they're looked up in a fixed void-tag list, not inferred from having no children:
###
@assert Html_Render.render(br()) == '<br>', 'a void tag should render with no closing tag'
@assert Html_Format.render(input([Attribute('type', 'text')])) == '<input type="text">', 'a void tag with attributes should still render with no closing tag'


### css attaches a real Style_Rule tree, rendered as an embedded <style>

The third constructor argument, `css`, takes any css.disk struct (`demos/css.disk`) -- not a Dictionary of inline properties. Wherever it's attached, the formatter renders it as one more child: an embedded `<style>` block, right there in the tree:
###
@load 'disks/css.disk'

styled := div('Found supplies', [], Style_Rule(['.box'], [Property('color', 'red')]))
@assert Html_Render.render(styled) == '<div>Found supplies<style>.box{color:red;}</style></div>', 'a css.disk rule attached via css should render as an embedded <style> child'


### Html_Stats_Visitor

A separate visitor walks the same kind of tree counting things instead of rendering it -- node count, max depth, and every unique tag used. `.analyze` resets its own counters at the start of every call:
###
stats := Html_Stats_Visitor().analyze(page)
@assert stats.node_count == 3, 'node_count should count every Element in the tree, including the root'
@assert stats.max_depth == 1, 'max_depth should be how many levels deep the tree goes'
@assert stats.unique_tags() == [:div, :h1, :p], 'unique_tags should list every distinct tag used'


### Html_Sanitizer_Visitor

`.sanitize` returns a cleaned copy of the tree -- the original is untouched, since Elements are plain, immutable data. `script`/`iframe`/`object`/`embed` tags are dropped entirely, along with their children; `on*` attributes and `javascript:` `href`/`src` values are stripped from whatever's left:
###
dirty := div([button('Click', [Attribute('onclick', 'evil()')]), script('alert(1)')])
clean := Html_Sanitizer_Visitor().sanitize(dirty)
@assert Html_Render.render(clean) == '<div><button>Click</button></div>', 'sanitize should strip the dangerous attribute and drop the dangerous tag entirely'


### Html_Lint_Visitor

Flags void elements given children, an `<img>` missing `alt`, empty containers, and duplicate attribute names (a real possibility now that `attributes` is an ordered Array, not a Dictionary). `.lint` resets its own warnings at the start of every call:
###
questionable := div([
	img([Attribute('src', 'flare.png')]),
	div([]),
	span('ok', [Attribute('id', 'a'), Attribute('id', 'b')])
])

warnings := Html_Lint_Visitor().lint(questionable)
@assert warnings == [
	'<img> is missing an alt attribute',
	'<div> is empty',
	"<span> has a duplicate 'id' attribute"
], 'lint should flag all three problems, in the order the elements appear'
